      <h2 class="sx-casestudy__title">Gradient Film Solved the Conference Room Problem Without Touching the Walls</h2>
      <p class="sx-casestudy__lead">A Rittenhouse Square co-working space had a design problem common to modern Philadelphia commercial interiors: six glass-walled conference pods in an open floor plan that provided zero visual privacy for client meetings.</p>
      <p class="sx-casestudy__body">The tenant's design brief required a privacy solution that preserved the architectural openness of the space, maintained natural light, and looked intentional — not applied. Replacing the glass or adding solid partitions was off the table for a leased space. Solyx Gradient Films — fading from frosted at seated head height to clear at floor level — provided exactly that: a seated occupant inside the pod is visually separated from the floor; someone walking past sees a gradient architectural detail, not a retrofitted privacy fix.</p>
      <p class="sx-casestudy__body">Window Film Philadelphia completed all six pods in a single day with no disruption to the co-working space's operations. The gradient pattern was consistent across all panels — a precision requirement when multiple panels make up a single continuous glass wall. The result: a conference room privacy solution that the client's architect approved, the tenant loved, and the building owner accepted without modification to the lease terms.</p>
      <blockquote class="sx-casestudy__quote">
        <p>&ldquo;We had three conference room glass walls treated with Solyx gradient film. It went from a fishbowl to a professional, private space — our tenants love it. The gradient effect looks intentional and architectural, not like a film fix.&rdquo;</p>
        <footer>David Kim &mdash; Property Manager, Center City Office Tower</footer>
      </blockquote>
